        <OtherAbstract Type="PIP" Language="eng">
            <AbstractText>An opinion on the prescription of oral contraceptives (OC) to women over the age of 34 who have predisposing risk factors for ischaemic heart disease and thromboembolism is presented. The age factor by itself is not considered an adequate criterion for contraindicating OC use, especially when evaluated against the risk of pregnancy for this age group. Long-term users of OCs are likely to be poorly motivated to use other contraceptive methods if they arbitrarily stop use at the age of 40, as the U.S. Food and Drug Administration recommends they do. The legal implications of the recommendation would likely deter many doctors from prescribing OCs to patients in this age group. Generally, OCs are contraindicated if plasma cholesterol levels are above 270 mg/100 ml. This test, along with the standard tests and screening procedures, should be administered to all women over the age of 40 with predisposing risk factors to thromboembolic events. Since Type 2 hyperlipoproteinemia is the only risk factor not easily detected during examination, it is recommended that the range of those authorized to dispense OCs should be extended to non-medical personnel.</AbstractText>
        </OtherAbstract>
